            "<strong>Culture</strong> is the learned, shared pattern of beliefs, values, language and behaviour that a group passes on — and it decides what a Ghanaian family calls illness, what it believes caused it, whom it consults first and whether it accepts what the nurse offers. Three kinds of causation live side by side in most communities. <strong>Natural</strong> causes — mosquitoes, bad food, cold, heredity, 'too much sugar' — explain the ordinary. <strong>Supernatural</strong> causes — witchcraft, a curse, ancestors offended, a shrine neglected, an evil eye on a pregnant woman — explain the sudden, the severe, the recurrent and the strange: convulsions, epilepsy, mental illness, infertility, a child who sickens as soon as the mother conceives again, the newborn illness the Akan call <em>asram</em>. <strong>Social</strong> causes — a broken taboo, adultery in pregnancy, a quarrel in the family — explain misfortune that needs reconciliation rather than tablets. The cause decides the cure: a natural illness goes to the chemical seller and the clinic, a spiritual one to the herbalist, the priest, the mallam or the prayer camp, and most families use all of them in sequence or at once. The usual <strong>pathway</strong> runs home remedy → chemical seller or drug peddler → traditional or faith healer → facility, often arriving at the fourth step late, poor and with a complication — and the pathway is not stupidity; it is cost, distance, previous experience, who in the family decides, and whether the facility has ever treated the family with respect.",
            "<strong>Cultural competence</strong> is the knowledge, attitude and skill to care well across these differences; <strong>cultural humility</strong> is the recognition that you never finish learning and that the patient is the expert on her own culture; <strong>cultural safety</strong> is judged by the recipient — care is safe when the person feels her identity and beliefs were respected, whatever the nurse intended. The opposite is <strong>ethnocentrism</strong>, judging another culture by the standards of your own, and nurses trained in the biomedical culture do it daily without noticing. Leininger's three modes keep the balance: <strong>preserve</strong> the practices that help or do no harm, <strong>accommodate</strong> or negotiate the ones that can be made safe, and <strong>repattern</strong> only the ones that injure. The practical tool is the <strong>explanatory model interview</strong>: what do you call this problem; what do you think caused it; why did it start when it did; what does it do to you; how severe is it; what treatment do you think you should receive; what do you hope for from it; what do you fear most about it? Ten minutes of these questions tell you whether the mother will give the tablets you are about to prescribe.",
