            "A CHPS zone runs on <strong>registers</strong>: the household register from which the community profile and every denominator come; the antenatal, delivery and postnatal registers; the child welfare and immunisation registers with their tally sheets; the family planning register; the consulting-room and outreach registers; the home-visit record; the tuberculosis, HIV, chronic-disease and nutrition programme registers; the births and deaths record; the referral book; and the <strong>stock cards</strong> and temperature chart. From them, each month, the nurse compiles the <strong>monthly report</strong> that the sub-district enters into <strong>DHIMS2</strong>, Ghana's instance of the DHIS2 health information system, and from which the district, the region and the national level see what happened in her zone — or do not, if the report is late, incomplete or wrong. Data quality has four tests: <strong>completeness</strong> (every facility and every data element reported), <strong>timeliness</strong> (by the deadline), <strong>accuracy</strong> (the register, the tally and the report agree — the supervisor's data-quality check counts the register and compares) and <strong>consistency</strong> (no impossible jumps, no coverage of 140 per cent, Penta 3 not higher than Penta 1). Electronic tools — the tablet-based tracker for antenatal and child records, the electronic IDSR — do not change the principles; they make the errors faster.",
            "Data become information when they are turned into <strong>indicators</strong> and displayed: the proportion of pregnant women booking before 12 weeks and attending four and eight contacts; skilled delivery; the postnatal visit within 48 hours; Penta 1 and Penta 3 coverage and the <strong>dropout</strong>; measles–rubella 2; family planning acceptors and couple-years of protection; the outpatient attendance per capita; the malaria test positivity rate; the tuberculosis case notification and treatment success; the defaulter rate in each programme; the number of households with latrines; the deaths by cause and the maternal deaths. The <strong>monitoring chart</strong> on the compound wall — cumulative monthly doses against the annual target — tells the nurse in March whether December will be met, and the quarterly <strong>data review meeting</strong> with the CHMC and the volunteers, with the community scorecard, turns the numbers into decisions. The honest use of data is a professional obligation: a target is a plan, not a figure to be reached by the pen; a coverage above 100 per cent is a question about the denominator, not a success; a zero report means zero cases, not an empty form; and the nurse who inflates her antenatal figures to look good deprives her zone of the outreach the true figure would have justified. Health records are confidential and protected by the Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) as well as by professional duty.",
            "Every birth and every death in the zone should be <strong>registered</strong>. Under the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 2020 (Act 1027) birth registration is free within the first twelve months and is the child's title to a name, a nationality, the NHIS, school and inheritance; the nurse's record of the birth and the weighing card start the process, and the registrar's agent visits or the family attends the district office. A death at a facility needs a medical certificate of cause of death; a death at home is reported to the registrar, with the nurse's <strong>verbal autopsy</strong> supplying the probable cause for the health system; a sudden, unnatural or suspicious death — including a death at a healer's premises — is reported to the police and the coroner before burial. Ghana's vital statistics are only as good as the zone's completeness.",
